{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Internal service tokens signed with public Cognito user pool ID allow full authentication bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Internal service tokens signed with public Cognito user pool ID allow full authentication bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In Cognito auth mode, internal service tokens (`internal.<data>.<sig>`) are HMAC-signed with the Cognito user pool ID, which is a public, non-secret identifier. Anyone who knows the pool ID (exposed in Cognito hosted-UI URLs and returned by the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint) can forge an internal token impersonating any user, organization, and role, bypassing all authentication."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In Cognito auth mode, internal service tokens (`internal.<data>.<sig>`) are HMAC-signed with the Cognito user pool ID, which is a public, non-secret identifier. Anyone who knows the pool ID (exposed in Cognito hosted-UI URLs and returned by the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint) can forge an internal token impersonating any user, organization, and role, bypassing all authentication.\n\nComplete authentication bypass in Cognito mode: an unauthenticated attacker becomes any user in any organization with any role (e.g. `owner`), gaining read/write access to all tenant data, agent execution, and administrative operations the platform offers. The forged principal is fully trusted by `authenticate()` — no profile lookup or membership check is applied to internal tokens.\n\nReplace the internal-token secret with a high-entropy secret independent of the user pool ID — generate a dedicated random secret at deploy time (as deploy.sh already does for JWT_SECRET) and inject it via environment variable. Reject any token whose secret material is missing or equals a known non-secret value. Additionally, replace the `!==` HMAC comparison at auth.ts:121 with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, and consider binding internal tokens to the issuing host/session so they cannot be replayed from outside.",
                "markdown": "In Cognito auth mode, internal service tokens (`internal.<data>.<sig>`) are HMAC-signed with the Cognito user pool ID, which is a public, non-secret identifier. Anyone who knows the pool ID (exposed in Cognito hosted-UI URLs and returned by the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint) can forge an internal token impersonating any user, organization, and role, bypassing all authentication.\n\nComplete authentication bypass in Cognito mode: an unauthenticated attacker becomes any user in any organization with any role (e.g. `owner`), gaining read/write access to all tenant data, agent execution, and administrative operations the platform offers. The forged principal is fully trusted by `authenticate()` — no profile lookup or membership check is applied to internal tokens.\n\nReplace the internal-token secret with a high-entropy secret independent of the user pool ID — generate a dedicated random secret at deploy time (as deploy.sh already does for JWT_SECRET) and inject it via environment variable. Reject any token whose secret material is missing or equals a known non-secret value. Additionally, replace the `!==` HMAC comparison at auth.ts:121 with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, and consider binding internal tokens to the issuing host/session so they cannot be replayed from outside."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-307",
              "name": "No rate limiting or lockout on local login, plus seeded default admin credentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No rate limiting or lockout on local login, plus seeded default admin credentials"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The local-mode login endpoint performs unauthenticated password attempts with no rate limiting, account lockout, CAPTCHA, or attempt counter. Combined with the seeded default credentials, an attacker can take over the admin account."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The local-mode login endpoint performs unauthenticated password attempts with no rate limiting, account lockout, CAPTCHA, or attempt counter. Combined with the seeded default credentials, an attacker can take over the admin account.\n\nAccount takeover of the seeded admin (`admin@example.com`, owner role) on any deployment that ran the seed script without changing the password, and of any weak local-mode password via online brute force. Owner role grants full administrative control of the platform's data.\n\nAdd per-account and per-IP rate limiting and temporary lockout to `POST /api/auth/login` and `POST /api/auth/register` (e.g. Redis-backed counter, 429 responses). Force a password change on first login for seeded accounts, or generate a random initial password instead of `admin123`. Reject common passwords at registration.",
                "markdown": "The local-mode login endpoint performs unauthenticated password attempts with no rate limiting, account lockout, CAPTCHA, or attempt counter. Combined with the seeded default credentials, an attacker can take over the admin account.\n\nAccount takeover of the seeded admin (`admin@example.com`, owner role) on any deployment that ran the seed script without changing the password, and of any weak local-mode password via online brute force. Owner role grants full administrative control of the platform's data.\n\nAdd per-account and per-IP rate limiting and temporary lockout to `POST /api/auth/login` and `POST /api/auth/register` (e.g. Redis-backed counter, 429 responses). Force a password change on first login for seeded accounts, or generate a random initial password instead of `admin123`. Reject common passwords at registration."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-307",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/307.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-598",
              "name": "JWT accepted via ?token= query parameter leaks session tokens into logs and referrers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWT accepted via ?token= query parameter leaks session tokens into logs and referrers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The authentication middleware accepts JWTs via a `?token=` query parameter, causing full session tokens to appear in URLs — logged by the request logger, stored in server/proxy access logs, browser history, and leaked via Referer headers to any third-party resource on the page."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The authentication middleware accepts JWTs via a `?token=` query parameter, causing full session tokens to appear in URLs — logged by the request logger, stored in server/proxy access logs, browser history, and leaked via Referer headers to any third-party resource on the page.\n\nSession token disclosure through logs (backend.log, pino output, reverse-proxy/CDN access logs), browser history, and Referer leakage to third-party origins. A captured token grants full authenticated API access for up to its 24h lifetime.\n\nRestrict query-param auth to the specific endpoints that need it (SSE/EventSource under /api/chat and image/avatar routes), reject it elsewhere, and prefer short-lived single-purpose tokens for those flows. Redact query strings containing tokens from the request logger, and set a strict Referrer-Policy on HTML responses.",
                "markdown": "The authentication middleware accepts JWTs via a `?token=` query parameter, causing full session tokens to appear in URLs — logged by the request logger, stored in server/proxy access logs, browser history, and leaked via Referer headers to any third-party resource on the page.\n\nSession token disclosure through logs (backend.log, pino output, reverse-proxy/CDN access logs), browser history, and Referer leakage to third-party origins. A captured token grants full authenticated API access for up to its 24h lifetime.\n\nRestrict query-param auth to the specific endpoints that need it (SSE/EventSource under /api/chat and image/avatar routes), reject it elsewhere, and prefer short-lived single-purpose tokens for those flows. Redact query strings containing tokens from the request logger, and set a strict Referrer-Policy on HTML responses."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "2.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-598",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-284",
              "name": "Cognito first-login email binding enables account takeover via unverified email claim",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cognito first-login email binding enables account takeover via unverified email claim"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "On first Cognito login, the backend binds a verified id_token to an existing local profile by matching the token's email claim against `profiles.username` and reassigns all of that profile's rows (memberships, chats, API keys, workflows) to the Cognito sub. If the user pool permits accounts whose email claim is not verified or is attacker-controlled, an attacker can take over any existing profile — including the admin — without knowing its password."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "On first Cognito login, the backend binds a verified id_token to an existing local profile by matching the token's email claim against `profiles.username` and reassigns all of that profile's rows (memberships, chats, API keys, workflows) to the Cognito sub. If the user pool permits accounts whose email claim is not verified or is attacker-controlled, an attacker can take over any existing profile — including the admin — without knowing its password.\n\nPre-authentication account takeover of any profile whose username equals an email the attacker can claim in the Cognito pool — including the seeded `admin@example.com` owner — inheriting all memberships, roles, chat history, and API keys.\n\nOnly bind by email when the id_token's `email_verified` claim is true (reject otherwise), require the pool to enforce unique verified emails, and require an explicit invitation/admin confirmation before migrating rows to a new identity. Consider replacing row reassignment with a stable identity mapping table.",
                "markdown": "On first Cognito login, the backend binds a verified id_token to an existing local profile by matching the token's email claim against `profiles.username` and reassigns all of that profile's rows (memberships, chats, API keys, workflows) to the Cognito sub. If the user pool permits accounts whose email claim is not verified or is attacker-controlled, an attacker can take over any existing profile — including the admin — without knowing its password.\n\nPre-authentication account takeover of any profile whose username equals an email the attacker can claim in the Cognito pool — including the seeded `admin@example.com` owner — inheriting all memberships, roles, chat history, and API keys.\n\nOnly bind by email when the id_token's `email_verified` claim is true (reject otherwise), require the pool to enforce unique verified emails, and require an explicit invitation/admin confirmation before migrating rows to a new identity. Consider replacing row reassignment with a stable identity mapping table."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-284",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/284.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-942",
              "name": "CORS wildcard origin combined with credentials:true",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CORS wildcard origin combined with credentials:true"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "CORS is configured with `credentials: true` together with a wildcard origin policy (default `CORS_ORIGIN=*` reflects any Origin with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <origin>` plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`), violating the CORS specification's intent and enabling credentialed cross-origin access if any cookie-based or ambient-credential flow is ever introduced."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "CORS is configured with `credentials: true` together with a wildcard origin policy (default `CORS_ORIGIN=*` reflects any Origin with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <origin>` plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`), violating the CORS specification's intent and enabling credentialed cross-origin access if any cookie-based or ambient-credential flow is ever introduced.\n\nWith the current Bearer-token architecture the practical impact is limited. It becomes a real credential/data leakage vector the moment any cookie-based session, HTTP Basic, TLS-client-cert, or ambient-credential mechanism is added. It also allows any origin to read non-credentialed responses, which matters for any future unauthenticated endpoints returning tenant data.\n\nDefault `CORS_ORIGIN` to an empty/strict value instead of `*`, refuse wildcard/reflection when `credentials: true` is set, and require an explicit origin allowlist in production. Add a startup validation that fails when `NODE_ENV=production` and `CORS_ORIGIN` is `*`.",
                "markdown": "CORS is configured with `credentials: true` together with a wildcard origin policy (default `CORS_ORIGIN=*` reflects any Origin with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <origin>` plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`), violating the CORS specification's intent and enabling credentialed cross-origin access if any cookie-based or ambient-credential flow is ever introduced.\n\nWith the current Bearer-token architecture the practical impact is limited. It becomes a real credential/data leakage vector the moment any cookie-based session, HTTP Basic, TLS-client-cert, or ambient-credential mechanism is added. It also allows any origin to read non-credentialed responses, which matters for any future unauthenticated endpoints returning tenant data.\n\nDefault `CORS_ORIGIN` to an empty/strict value instead of `*`, refuse wildcard/reflection when `credentials: true` is set, and require an explicit origin allowlist in production. Add a startup validation that fails when `NODE_ENV=production` and `CORS_ORIGIN` is `*`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-942",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/942.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-665",
              "name": "A2A routes compare raw API key against stored SHA-256 hash — feature always rejects valid keys (reliability defect)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A2A routes compare raw API key against stored SHA-256 hash — feature always rejects valid keys (reliability defect)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The A2A protocol routes validate bearer tokens by comparing the raw token value against the `key_hash` column, which stores a SHA-256 hash of the key. Every legitimate API key is therefore rejected, disabling the A2A feature. This is a functional/reliability defect, not an exploitable vulnerability."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The A2A protocol routes validate bearer tokens by comparing the raw token value against the `key_hash` column, which stores a SHA-256 hash of the key. Every legitimate API key is therefore rejected, disabling the A2A feature. This is a functional/reliability defect, not an exploitable vulnerability.\n\nNo security impact demonstrated: A2A endpoints (agent card, task send, task status) reject all valid keys and cannot be used. If \"fixed\" naively by storing raw keys, it would become one; the correct fix preserves hashing. This is a reliability/usability defect in an advertised feature.\n\nIn `validateApiKey` (src/routes/a2a.routes.ts:41-57), hash the bearer token with SHA-256 before querying `key_hash`, matching `apiKeyService.validateApiKey` (or reuse that service, which also handles expiry, caching, and rate limiting).",
                "markdown": "The A2A protocol routes validate bearer tokens by comparing the raw token value against the `key_hash` column, which stores a SHA-256 hash of the key. Every legitimate API key is therefore rejected, disabling the A2A feature. This is a functional/reliability defect, not an exploitable vulnerability.\n\nNo security impact demonstrated: A2A endpoints (agent card, task send, task status) reject all valid keys and cannot be used. If \"fixed\" naively by storing raw keys, it would become one; the correct fix preserves hashing. This is a reliability/usability defect in an advertised feature.\n\nIn `validateApiKey` (src/routes/a2a.routes.ts:41-57), hash the bearer token with SHA-256 before querying `key_hash`, matching `apiKeyService.validateApiKey` (or reuse that service, which also handles expiry, caching, and rate limiting)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-665",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/665.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0009",
              "name": "Unrestricted SSRF in workflow api_call action node (ActionExecutor and workflow-orchestrator)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted SSRF in workflow api_call action node (ActionExecutor and workflow-orchestrator)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workflow engine's `api_call` action node (both the legacy orchestrator and the V2 node-executor) fetches a user-authored URL with Node's global `fetch()` and no SSRF validation: no denylist for loopback/link-local/RFC1918 addresses, no scheme pinning, and redirects are followed by default. An authenticated org member who can author or edit a workflow — or anyone who can hit a public webhook trigger for such a workflow — can direct the backend to make arbitrary requests to internal services and cloud metadata endpoints."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workflow engine's `api_call` action node (both the legacy orchestrator and the V2 node-executor) fetches a user-authored URL with Node's global `fetch()` and no SSRF validation: no denylist for loopback/link-local/RFC1918 addresses, no scheme pinning, and redirects are followed by default. An authenticated org member who can author or edit a workflow — or anyone who can hit a public webhook trigger for such a workflow — can direct the backend to make arbitrary requests to internal services and cloud metadata endpoints.\n\nAn authenticated user (or an unauthenticated caller of a public webhook trigger for an existing workflow) can reach internal-only endpoints from the backend server: cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) to steal role credentials, internal admin panels, and unauthenticated internal services. Attacker-supplied headers and body are passed through to the fetch, and the response body is stored in workflow execution logs/output, so responses from internal endpoints can be exfiltrated back to the attacker. Redirect-following defeats first-hop allowlists and enables DNS-rebinding-style pivots.\n\nIntroduce a shared URL validation utility for all server-side fetches of user-influenced URLs: resolve the hostname and reject loopback (127.0.0.0/8, ::1), link-local (169.254.0.0/16, fe80::/10), RFC1918 ranges, and cloud metadata endpoints before connecting; pin the scheme to https (and http only if explicitly required); disable or re-validate redirects (fetch option `redirect: 'manual'` with the same validation applied to each hop); consider resolving DNS once and connecting to the validated IP to prevent DNS rebinding. Apply the same utility to the MCP server test connection, a2a remote endpoints, and any other user-supplied URL fetch.",
                "markdown": "The workflow engine's `api_call` action node (both the legacy orchestrator and the V2 node-executor) fetches a user-authored URL with Node's global `fetch()` and no SSRF validation: no denylist for loopback/link-local/RFC1918 addresses, no scheme pinning, and redirects are followed by default. An authenticated org member who can author or edit a workflow — or anyone who can hit a public webhook trigger for such a workflow — can direct the backend to make arbitrary requests to internal services and cloud metadata endpoints.\n\nAn authenticated user (or an unauthenticated caller of a public webhook trigger for an existing workflow) can reach internal-only endpoints from the backend server: cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) to steal role credentials, internal admin panels, and unauthenticated internal services. Attacker-supplied headers and body are passed through to the fetch, and the response body is stored in workflow execution logs/output, so responses from internal endpoints can be exfiltrated back to the attacker. Redirect-following defeats first-hop allowlists and enables DNS-rebinding-style pivots.\n\nIntroduce a shared URL validation utility for all server-side fetches of user-influenced URLs: resolve the hostname and reject loopback (127.0.0.0/8, ::1), link-local (169.254.0.0/16, fe80::/10), RFC1918 ranges, and cloud metadata endpoints before connecting; pin the scheme to https (and http only if explicitly required); disable or re-validate redirects (fetch option `redirect: 'manual'` with the same validation applied to each hop); consider resolving DNS once and connecting to the validated IP to prevent DNS rebinding. Apply the same utility to the MCP server test connection, a2a remote endpoints, and any other user-supplied URL fetch."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "name": "Unrestricted SSRF via MCP server connection test endpoint",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted SSRF via MCP server connection test endpoint"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The MCP server connection test (`POST /api/mcp/servers/:id/test`) performs a server-side `fetch()` to a fully user-controlled URL (the server's `host_address` or `config.url`) with no SSRF protections: no internal-IP denylist, no scheme restriction, and redirects followed by default. This gives any authenticated user a server-side request primitive to internal networks and cloud metadata."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The MCP server connection test (`POST /api/mcp/servers/:id/test`) performs a server-side `fetch()` to a fully user-controlled URL (the server's `host_address` or `config.url`) with no SSRF protections: no internal-IP denylist, no scheme restriction, and redirects followed by default. This gives any authenticated user a server-side request primitive to internal networks and cloud metadata.\n\nAuthenticated users can probe internal networks from the backend's network position: reach cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254), map internal services via status/latency oracles, and reach internal admin endpoints. The response body is not returned directly, but error messages (e.g. connection failures, status codes) distinguish open/closed services, forming an internal port scanner. The same `config.url`/`host_address` may also be fetched by other MCP client code paths with more request control.\n\nValidate the resolved IP of `config.url` / `host_address` against a blocklist (loopback, link-local including 169.254.169.254, RFC1918, IPv6 equivalents) before every server-side fetch of user-supplied URLs; pin the scheme to http(s); do not follow redirects (or re-validate each hop); resolve DNS once and connect to the validated IP to prevent DNS rebinding. Apply the shared validation utility across all fetch sites in the codebase.",
                "markdown": "The MCP server connection test (`POST /api/mcp/servers/:id/test`) performs a server-side `fetch()` to a fully user-controlled URL (the server's `host_address` or `config.url`) with no SSRF protections: no internal-IP denylist, no scheme restriction, and redirects followed by default. This gives any authenticated user a server-side request primitive to internal networks and cloud metadata.\n\nAuthenticated users can probe internal networks from the backend's network position: reach cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254), map internal services via status/latency oracles, and reach internal admin endpoints. The response body is not returned directly, but error messages (e.g. connection failures, status codes) distinguish open/closed services, forming an internal port scanner. The same `config.url`/`host_address` may also be fetched by other MCP client code paths with more request control.\n\nValidate the resolved IP of `config.url` / `host_address` against a blocklist (loopback, link-local including 169.254.169.254, RFC1918, IPv6 equivalents) before every server-side fetch of user-supplied URLs; pin the scheme to http(s); do not follow redirects (or re-validate each hop); resolve DNS once and connect to the validated IP to prevent DNS rebinding. Apply the shared validation utility across all fetch sites in the codebase."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "name": "Webhook trigger ignores stored secret — unauthenticated workflow execution and public execution-state disclosure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Webhook trigger ignores stored secret — unauthenticated workflow execution and public execution-state disclosure"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The public webhook trigger endpoint accepts unauthenticated POSTs and never verifies the stored webhook secret, even when one was generated at creation time. The `secret_hash` column is written but no code reads it — a repository-wide search shows zero verification logic. Anyone who learns or guesses a webhook ID can trigger the associated workflow with attacker-chosen variables, and query its execution state through a companion public status endpoint."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The public webhook trigger endpoint accepts unauthenticated POSTs and never verifies the stored webhook secret, even when one was generated at creation time. The `secret_hash` column is written but no code reads it — a repository-wide search shows zero verification logic. Anyone who learns or guesses a webhook ID can trigger the associated workflow with attacker-chosen variables, and query its execution state through a companion public status endpoint.\n\nAnyone with a webhook ID can: (1) execute the target organization's workflows on demand with arbitrary input variables, consuming LLM/agent compute and triggering whatever side effects the workflow's nodes perform (API calls, document generation, message sending); (2) enumerate/infer workflow behavior through the public status endpoint which exposes node-level execution detail and error messages without authentication. Since webhook IDs appear in URLs (logs, browser history, referrer headers), disclosure risk is realistic.\n\nVerify the webhook secret on every trigger: require an HMAC-SHA256 signature header (e.g. X-Webhook-Signature) over the raw request body, computed with the stored secret, compared with a timing-safe comparison against the secret hash; reject triggers for webhooks with no configured secret unless the operator explicitly opts the webhook into unauthenticated mode. Add replay protection (timestamp + nonce or timestamp freshness in the signature base). Require authentication (or a capability token) on the /v1/webhook/status endpoint and scope it to the webhook's organization. Add rate limiting to the public trigger route.",
                "markdown": "The public webhook trigger endpoint accepts unauthenticated POSTs and never verifies the stored webhook secret, even when one was generated at creation time. The `secret_hash` column is written but no code reads it — a repository-wide search shows zero verification logic. Anyone who learns or guesses a webhook ID can trigger the associated workflow with attacker-chosen variables, and query its execution state through a companion public status endpoint.\n\nAnyone with a webhook ID can: (1) execute the target organization's workflows on demand with arbitrary input variables, consuming LLM/agent compute and triggering whatever side effects the workflow's nodes perform (API calls, document generation, message sending); (2) enumerate/infer workflow behavior through the public status endpoint which exposes node-level execution detail and error messages without authentication. Since webhook IDs appear in URLs (logs, browser history, referrer headers), disclosure risk is realistic.\n\nVerify the webhook secret on every trigger: require an HMAC-SHA256 signature header (e.g. X-Webhook-Signature) over the raw request body, computed with the stored secret, compared with a timing-safe comparison against the secret hash; reject triggers for webhooks with no configured secret unless the operator explicitly opts the webhook into unauthenticated mode. Add replay protection (timestamp + nonce or timestamp freshness in the signature base). Require authentication (or a capability token) on the /v1/webhook/status endpoint and scope it to the webhook's organization. Add rate limiting to the public trigger route."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "name": "OAuth client secrets encrypted with hardcoded default key; provider upsert accepts arbitrary provider names",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "OAuth client secrets encrypted with hardcoded default key; provider upsert accepts arbitrary provider names"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Per-org OAuth client secrets are \"encrypted\" with AES-256-GCM using a key derived from `CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, which defaults to a hardcoded string committed to the repository. Any party who obtains DB rows (or a DB backup) and the public source can decrypt every org's client secrets. Additionally, `oauth-provider-config.service.ts` `upsert` accepts arbitrary provider names with no allowlist — combined with the fact that `getProviderConfig` only hardcodes token URLs for google/salesforce, the door is open for a future per-provider URL field to become fully attacker-controlled."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Per-org OAuth client secrets are \"encrypted\" with AES-256-GCM using a key derived from `CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, which defaults to a hardcoded string committed to the repository. Any party who obtains DB rows (or a DB backup) and the public source can decrypt every org's client secrets. Additionally, `oauth-provider-config.service.ts` `upsert` accepts arbitrary provider names with no allowlist — combined with the fact that `getProviderConfig` only hardcodes token URLs for google/salesforce, the door is open for a future per-provider URL field to become fully attacker-controlled.\n\nAny attacker with database read access (or a leaked backup, or a SQL injection elsewhere) can decrypt all stored OAuth client secrets despite the at-rest encryption claim, then use them to impersonate the org's OAuth applications. Because the encryption key default is public in the repo, the encryption provides no protection in any deployment that has not set CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY.\n\nRemove the hardcoded fallback for CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY and fail closed at startup when it is unset (or generate and persist a random key on first boot); use a per-value random salt for the scrypt derivation instead of the constant 'salt'; add a provider allowlist to the upsert route; rotate all stored client secrets once a proper key is in place since existing ciphertexts are recoverable with the old key.",
                "markdown": "Per-org OAuth client secrets are \"encrypted\" with AES-256-GCM using a key derived from `CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, which defaults to a hardcoded string committed to the repository. Any party who obtains DB rows (or a DB backup) and the public source can decrypt every org's client secrets. Additionally, `oauth-provider-config.service.ts` `upsert` accepts arbitrary provider names with no allowlist — combined with the fact that `getProviderConfig` only hardcodes token URLs for google/salesforce, the door is open for a future per-provider URL field to become fully attacker-controlled.\n\nAny attacker with database read access (or a leaked backup, or a SQL injection elsewhere) can decrypt all stored OAuth client secrets despite the at-rest encryption claim, then use them to impersonate the org's OAuth applications. Because the encryption key default is public in the repo, the encryption provides no protection in any deployment that has not set CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY.\n\nRemove the hardcoded fallback for CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY and fail closed at startup when it is unset (or generate and persist a random key on first boot); use a per-value random salt for the scrypt derivation instead of the constant 'salt'; add a provider allowlist to the upsert route; rotate all stored client secrets once a proper key is in place since existing ciphertexts are recoverable with the old key."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Path traversal to arbitrary file write via unvalidated skill name in workspace provisioning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal to arbitrary file write via unvalidated skill name in workspace provisioning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Skill names are never validated or sanitized. A name containing `../` (fully attacker-controlled via the skill zip upload endpoint or marketplace install reference) is joined into filesystem paths without any containment check in `WorkspaceManager.installSkillToWorkspace` and `WorkspaceManager.downloadSkill`, so skill provisioning copies attacker-controlled files (arbitrary content from the uploaded zip) to arbitrary directories outside the session workspace — an arbitrary file write with controlled content on the backend host."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Skill names are never validated or sanitized. A name containing `../` (fully attacker-controlled via the skill zip upload endpoint or marketplace install reference) is joined into filesystem paths without any containment check in `WorkspaceManager.installSkillToWorkspace` and `WorkspaceManager.downloadSkill`, so skill provisioning copies attacker-controlled files (arbitrary content from the uploaded zip) to arbitrary directories outside the session workspace — an arbitrary file write with controlled content on the backend host.\n\nArbitrary file write with attacker-controlled content anywhere the backend process can write. Because the zip upload controls both the name and the file contents of the skill, an authenticated user can overwrite backend data, configuration, or user-level execution hooks (e.g. `~/.bashrc`, systemd user units, cron entries, or other service files writable by the backend user), leading to code execution on the backend host. The S3 mirror at skill-marketplace.routes.ts:197 builds `destKey` from the same unsanitized name, so the traversal additionally escapes the per-session S3 key prefix and can overwrite arbitrary workspace-bucket objects in agentcore deployments.\n\nValidate skill names at the trust boundary: reject any skill name containing path separators, `..`, backslashes, absolute-path prefixes, or characters outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` when a skill is created (`installFromZip`, `install`, `createScopeLevelSkill`, scope-generator confirm). Independently, enforce containment at the sink: in `installSkillToWorkspace` and `downloadSkill`, resolve the target and verify `resolved === skillsDir || resolved.startsWith(skillsDir + path.sep)` before `cp`/`writeFile` (the same boundary-corrected check should replace the separator-less `startsWith` used elsewhere in the file). Apply the identical validation to the S3 key construction in the marketplace and enterprise-skill sync paths so a traversal name cannot escape the per-session prefix.",
                "markdown": "Skill names are never validated or sanitized. A name containing `../` (fully attacker-controlled via the skill zip upload endpoint or marketplace install reference) is joined into filesystem paths without any containment check in `WorkspaceManager.installSkillToWorkspace` and `WorkspaceManager.downloadSkill`, so skill provisioning copies attacker-controlled files (arbitrary content from the uploaded zip) to arbitrary directories outside the session workspace — an arbitrary file write with controlled content on the backend host.\n\nArbitrary file write with attacker-controlled content anywhere the backend process can write. Because the zip upload controls both the name and the file contents of the skill, an authenticated user can overwrite backend data, configuration, or user-level execution hooks (e.g. `~/.bashrc`, systemd user units, cron entries, or other service files writable by the backend user), leading to code execution on the backend host. The S3 mirror at skill-marketplace.routes.ts:197 builds `destKey` from the same unsanitized name, so the traversal additionally escapes the per-session S3 key prefix and can overwrite arbitrary workspace-bucket objects in agentcore deployments.\n\nValidate skill names at the trust boundary: reject any skill name containing path separators, `..`, backslashes, absolute-path prefixes, or characters outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` when a skill is created (`installFromZip`, `install`, `createScopeLevelSkill`, scope-generator confirm). Independently, enforce containment at the sink: in `installSkillToWorkspace` and `downloadSkill`, resolve the target and verify `resolved === skillsDir || resolved.startsWith(skillsDir + path.sep)` before `cp`/`writeFile` (the same boundary-corrected check should replace the separator-less `startsWith` used elsewhere in the file). Apply the identical validation to the S3 key construction in the marketplace and enterprise-skill sync paths so a traversal name cannot escape the per-session prefix."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-78",
              "name": "Shell command injection via unvalidated skill name in AgentCore container provisioning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Shell command injection via unvalidated skill name in AgentCore container provisioning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In AgentCore runtime mode, skill names are interpolated unsanitized into shell commands executed inside the AgentCore runtime container. `runCommand(sessionId, \\`mkdir -p /workspace/.claude/skills/${skill.name}\\`)` builds a bash command from a skill name that is fully attacker-controlled (derived from an uploaded zip's directory name or the marketplace install reference), and the command wrapper only escapes single quotes and backslashes — so `;`, `$()`, and backticks pass through to `/bin/bash -c`, giving arbitrary command execution in the runtime container."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In AgentCore runtime mode, skill names are interpolated unsanitized into shell commands executed inside the AgentCore runtime container. `runCommand(sessionId, \\`mkdir -p /workspace/.claude/skills/${skill.name}\\`)` builds a bash command from a skill name that is fully attacker-controlled (derived from an uploaded zip's directory name or the marketplace install reference), and the command wrapper only escapes single quotes and backslashes — so `;`, `$()`, and backticks pass through to `/bin/bash -c`, giving arbitrary command execution in the runtime container.\n\nArbitrary command execution inside the AgentCore runtime container as the container's user: reading or modifying any workspace file across sessions hosted by that container, exfiltrating container credentials (including the `WORKSPACE_S3_REGION`-configured AWS credentials used by the in-container S3 client), and pivoting to the workspace S3 bucket. Container-level RCE, contained by the AgentCore runtime sandbox boundary.\n\nRoute every directory-creating operation through the existing `sanitizePath`/`writeFile` helpers instead of composing raw `mkdir -p` command strings: replace the `runCommand(sessionId, \\`mkdir -p /workspace/.claude/skills/${name}\\`)` calls at skill-marketplace.routes.ts:158 and enterprise-skill.service.ts:338 with a dedicated `mkdirDirectory` wrapper that applies `sanitizePath` (which already rejects `/`, `..`, and all shell metacharacters) or, better, rejects any name not matching `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$`. Enforce the same validation at skill creation so a hostile name can never be persisted. Optionally harden `shellEscape` to full POSIX single-quoting (`'...'` with `'\\''` for embedded quotes) so no caller can regress the wrapper.",
                "markdown": "In AgentCore runtime mode, skill names are interpolated unsanitized into shell commands executed inside the AgentCore runtime container. `runCommand(sessionId, \\`mkdir -p /workspace/.claude/skills/${skill.name}\\`)` builds a bash command from a skill name that is fully attacker-controlled (derived from an uploaded zip's directory name or the marketplace install reference), and the command wrapper only escapes single quotes and backslashes — so `;`, `$()`, and backticks pass through to `/bin/bash -c`, giving arbitrary command execution in the runtime container.\n\nArbitrary command execution inside the AgentCore runtime container as the container's user: reading or modifying any workspace file across sessions hosted by that container, exfiltrating container credentials (including the `WORKSPACE_S3_REGION`-configured AWS credentials used by the in-container S3 client), and pivoting to the workspace S3 bucket. Container-level RCE, contained by the AgentCore runtime sandbox boundary.\n\nRoute every directory-creating operation through the existing `sanitizePath`/`writeFile` helpers instead of composing raw `mkdir -p` command strings: replace the `runCommand(sessionId, \\`mkdir -p /workspace/.claude/skills/${name}\\`)` calls at skill-marketplace.routes.ts:158 and enterprise-skill.service.ts:338 with a dedicated `mkdirDirectory` wrapper that applies `sanitizePath` (which already rejects `/`, `..`, and all shell metacharacters) or, better, rejects any name not matching `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$`. Enforce the same validation at skill creation so a hostile name can never be persisted. Optionally harden `shellEscape` to full POSIX single-quoting (`'...'` with `'\\''` for embedded quotes) so no caller can regress the wrapper."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0015",
              "name": "Member-configurable stdio MCP servers allow arbitrary command execution on the backend host",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Member-configurable stdio MCP servers allow arbitrary command execution on the backend host"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any authenticated organization member can register an MCP server whose stdio configuration contains an arbitrary command, arguments, and environment variables. That configuration flows unvalidated into the Claude Agent SDK's MCP server spawn configuration, so the next agent conversation in a scope with that server attached executes the command as a subprocess of the backend server process."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any authenticated organization member can register an MCP server whose stdio configuration contains an arbitrary command, arguments, and environment variables. That configuration flows unvalidated into the Claude Agent SDK's MCP server spawn configuration, so the next agent conversation in a scope with that server attached executes the command as a subprocess of the backend server process.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the backend host with the backend process's privileges, including the full `process.env` (AWS Bedrock keys, JWT secret in local mode are injected per claude-agent buildOptions; any DB/env secrets reachable by the process). A member-role user — the lowest privileged org role — reaches host-level code execution, i.e. a full vertical privilege escalation beyond the multi-tenant application boundary.\n\nRestrict member-configurable MCP servers to curated transports: allow only http/sse MCP URLs against an operator-approved allowlist, and gate stdio server registration (command/args/env) behind an admin/owner-only permission with an executable allowlist (e.g. node/npx with pinned, verified package identifiers). Validate `config.command` against the allowlist in `createMcpServer`/`updateMcpServer` before persisting, and re-validate in `parseMCPServerConfig` and `generateSettings` so stored rows cannot bypass the check. Reject `host_address` strings that tokenize into arbitrary executables when no structured config exists.",
                "markdown": "Any authenticated organization member can register an MCP server whose stdio configuration contains an arbitrary command, arguments, and environment variables. That configuration flows unvalidated into the Claude Agent SDK's MCP server spawn configuration, so the next agent conversation in a scope with that server attached executes the command as a subprocess of the backend server process.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the backend host with the backend process's privileges, including the full `process.env` (AWS Bedrock keys, JWT secret in local mode are injected per claude-agent buildOptions; any DB/env secrets reachable by the process). A member-role user — the lowest privileged org role — reaches host-level code execution, i.e. a full vertical privilege escalation beyond the multi-tenant application boundary.\n\nRestrict member-configurable MCP servers to curated transports: allow only http/sse MCP URLs against an operator-approved allowlist, and gate stdio server registration (command/args/env) behind an admin/owner-only permission with an executable allowlist (e.g. node/npx with pinned, verified package identifiers). Validate `config.command` against the allowlist in `createMcpServer`/`updateMcpServer` before persisting, and re-validate in `parseMCPServerConfig` and `generateSettings` so stored rows cannot bypass the check. Reject `host_address` strings that tokenize into arbitrary executables when no structured config exists."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-tenant IDOR in knowledge-base service: all operations keyed by primary key without organization scoping",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-tenant IDOR in knowledge-base service: all operations keyed by primary key without organization scoping"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The knowledge-base service queries and mutates knowledge bases, knowledge files, and scope-knowledge bindings strictly by primary key with no organization_id predicate, while the routes pass only the attacker-supplied id. Any authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, soft-delete, or re-bind another tenant's knowledge bases and delete their files by ID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The knowledge-base service queries and mutates knowledge bases, knowledge files, and scope-knowledge bindings strictly by primary key with no organization_id predicate, while the routes pass only the attacker-supplied id. Any authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, soft-delete, or re-bind another tenant's knowledge bases and delete their files by ID.\n\nCross-tenant (cross-organization) read of knowledge base metadata and file listings, unauthorized rename/soft-delete of other orgs' knowledge bases, deletion of other orgs' knowledge files, and binding of another org's knowledge base to an arbitrary scope — integrity and confidentiality violation of tenant data.\n\nThread the caller's organization_id through knowledgeBaseService.findById/update/delete/bindToScope/unbindFromScope/getScopeBindings and the file operations, and enforce it as a predicate in every Prisma where clause (mirroring BaseRepository.findById). At the route layer, reject ids that do not belong to request.user.orgId before any read or write.",
                "markdown": "The knowledge-base service queries and mutates knowledge bases, knowledge files, and scope-knowledge bindings strictly by primary key with no organization_id predicate, while the routes pass only the attacker-supplied id. Any authenticated member of any organization can read, rename, soft-delete, or re-bind another tenant's knowledge bases and delete their files by ID.\n\nCross-tenant (cross-organization) read of knowledge base metadata and file listings, unauthorized rename/soft-delete of other orgs' knowledge bases, deletion of other orgs' knowledge files, and binding of another org's knowledge base to an arbitrary scope — integrity and confidentiality violation of tenant data.\n\nThread the caller's organization_id through knowledgeBaseService.findById/update/delete/bindToScope/unbindFromScope/getScopeBindings and the file operations, and enforce it as a predicate in every Prisma where clause (mirroring BaseRepository.findById). At the route layer, reject ids that do not belong to request.user.orgId before any read or write."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "name": "Agent runtime shell guard is a bypassable denylist under permissionMode bypassPermissions (prompt-injection path to command execution)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Agent runtime shell guard is a bypassable denylist under permissionMode bypassPermissions (prompt-injection path to command execution)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Claude agent runtime runs with `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`, granting the model Bash, Write, Edit, and WebFetch by default. The only compensating control on shell commands is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed via variable indirection, base64 wrapping, interpreters, or relative paths. Untrusted content that reaches an agent conversation (IM messages, attached files, retrieved documents) can therefore drive arbitrary command execution, file writes outside intended flows, and outbound exfiltration under the agent's credentials — with no human-confirmation gate."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Claude agent runtime runs with `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`, granting the model Bash, Write, Edit, and WebFetch by default. The only compensating control on shell commands is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed via variable indirection, base64 wrapping, interpreters, or relative paths. Untrusted content that reaches an agent conversation (IM messages, attached files, retrieved documents) can therefore drive arbitrary command execution, file writes outside intended flows, and outbound exfiltration under the agent's credentials — with no human-confirmation gate.\n\nAn attacker who injects instructions into agent context (IM file content, chat, fetched web pages, skill files) gets arbitrary command execution in the agent's execution environment and exfiltration of any file the process can read (workspace files, and in local mode the backend process's environment). The agent holds a member-role platform JWT (AUTH_TOKEN, claude-agent.service.ts:479-485) and, in Bedrock mode, AWS credentials, so successful injection escalates to those credentials. There is no approval gate for destructive actions; the workflow humanApproval node exists only inside workflow executions, not chat/IM sessions.\n\nReplace the denylist with an allowlist-based execution policy: run agent sessions in a hardened sandbox (container/namespace with no ambient credentials, restricted egress), drop `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions` in favor of explicit per-tool approval or a strict allowlist of permitted command prefixes and workspace-relative paths resolved after shell-expansion; block network egress from Bash/WebFetch except to operator-configured destinations; and add a human-confirmation gate for destructive tool calls triggered from IM-sourced conversations. Do not pass the full `process.env` or a member-role platform JWT to the agent subprocess.",
                "markdown": "The Claude agent runtime runs with `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`, granting the model Bash, Write, Edit, and WebFetch by default. The only compensating control on shell commands is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed via variable indirection, base64 wrapping, interpreters, or relative paths. Untrusted content that reaches an agent conversation (IM messages, attached files, retrieved documents) can therefore drive arbitrary command execution, file writes outside intended flows, and outbound exfiltration under the agent's credentials — with no human-confirmation gate.\n\nAn attacker who injects instructions into agent context (IM file content, chat, fetched web pages, skill files) gets arbitrary command execution in the agent's execution environment and exfiltration of any file the process can read (workspace files, and in local mode the backend process's environment). The agent holds a member-role platform JWT (AUTH_TOKEN, claude-agent.service.ts:479-485) and, in Bedrock mode, AWS credentials, so successful injection escalates to those credentials. There is no approval gate for destructive actions; the workflow humanApproval node exists only inside workflow executions, not chat/IM sessions.\n\nReplace the denylist with an allowlist-based execution policy: run agent sessions in a hardened sandbox (container/namespace with no ambient credentials, restricted egress), drop `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions` in favor of explicit per-tool approval or a strict allowlist of permitted command prefixes and workspace-relative paths resolved after shell-expansion; block network egress from Bash/WebFetch except to operator-configured destinations; and add a human-confirmation gate for destructive tool calls triggered from IM-sourced conversations. Do not pass the full `process.env` or a member-role platform JWT to the agent subprocess."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0019",
              "name": "Cross-tenant BOLA: checkpoint resolve/resume and execution detail routes skip organization scoping",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-tenant BOLA: checkpoint resolve/resume and execution detail routes skip organization scoping"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Three workflow checkpoint/execution endpoints perform no organization check: the checkpoint resolve route, the execution status route in checkpoint.routes.ts, and the executor resume path. Any authenticated user of any organization can read another organization's paused execution details, resolve or cancel their human-approval checkpoints, and resume their workflows, by supplying the execution or checkpoint UUID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Three workflow checkpoint/execution endpoints perform no organization check: the checkpoint resolve route, the execution status route in checkpoint.routes.ts, and the executor resume path. Any authenticated user of any organization can read another organization's paused execution details, resolve or cancel their human-approval checkpoints, and resume their workflows, by supplying the execution or checkpoint UUID.\n\nCross-tenant integrity impact on workflow approval flows: a user from organization A can approve or cancel organization B's human-approval checkpoints — the exact control meant to gate destructive workflow segments — and force-resume or steer B's paused executions by supplying an arbitrary `scopeId` (the resume route passes the user-supplied scopeId to workspace provisioning and the executor, `checkpoint.routes.ts:66`). They can also read B's execution state, checkpoint configuration, and inputContext (which contains workflow variable values and prior node outputs). This defeats the tenant isolation invariant of the platform.\n\nAdd organization scoping to all three paths: in `checkpointService.resolve`, look up the checkpoint with `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })` (the org id is returned by the route's user context); in the checkpoint GET execution route and `workflowExecutorV2.resume`, filter `workflow_executions` by `organization_id` (and for resume, derive `scopeId` from the execution record instead of accepting it from the request body). Mirror the pattern already used in execution.routes.ts.",
                "markdown": "Three workflow checkpoint/execution endpoints perform no organization check: the checkpoint resolve route, the execution status route in checkpoint.routes.ts, and the executor resume path. Any authenticated user of any organization can read another organization's paused execution details, resolve or cancel their human-approval checkpoints, and resume their workflows, by supplying the execution or checkpoint UUID.\n\nCross-tenant integrity impact on workflow approval flows: a user from organization A can approve or cancel organization B's human-approval checkpoints — the exact control meant to gate destructive workflow segments — and force-resume or steer B's paused executions by supplying an arbitrary `scopeId` (the resume route passes the user-supplied scopeId to workspace provisioning and the executor, `checkpoint.routes.ts:66`). They can also read B's execution state, checkpoint configuration, and inputContext (which contains workflow variable values and prior node outputs). This defeats the tenant isolation invariant of the platform.\n\nAdd organization scoping to all three paths: in `checkpointService.resolve`, look up the checkpoint with `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })` (the org id is returned by the route's user context); in the checkpoint GET execution route and `workflowExecutorV2.resume`, filter `workflow_executions` by `organization_id` (and for resume, derive `scopeId` from the execution record instead of accepting it from the request body). Mirror the pattern already used in execution.routes.ts."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Unauthenticated, unscoped WebSocket gateway leaks any tenant's workflow execution events",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated, unscoped WebSocket gateway leaks any tenant's workflow execution events"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The WebSocket endpoint `/ws/executions` accepts unauthenticated connections and allows any client to subscribe to arbitrary workflow `executionId` values with no ownership or tenant check. Workflow node events — including full node output (`result.output`, e.g. LLM responses and tool results) — are then broadcast to that client, leaking any tenant's execution data to an unauthenticated network peer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The WebSocket endpoint `/ws/executions` accepts unauthenticated connections and allows any client to subscribe to arbitrary workflow `executionId` values with no ownership or tenant check. Workflow node events — including full node output (`result.output`, e.g. LLM responses and tool results) — are then broadcast to that client, leaking any tenant's execution data to an unauthenticated network peer.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who knows (or brute-forces/obtains) any execution UUID can stream that execution's live workflow events, including node results produced by LLM and tool nodes, across all organizations/tenants. This exposes other tenants' business data (workflow outputs, error messages, prompts echoed in results) in real time. It also lets an unauthenticated client hold arbitrary subscriptions, enabling resource consumption.\n\nAuthenticate the WebSocket upgrade: validate the JWT (Authorization header or a short-lived one-time ticket issued by an authenticated REST endpoint) inside the `/ws/executions` handler before accepting the connection, and reject unauthenticated upgrades. On `subscribe`, look up the execution by ID scoped to the authenticated user's organization (e.g. `workflowExecutionService.getExecution(executionId, request.user.orgId)`) and reject subscriptions to executions the org does not own. Consider also validating origin headers for browser clients.",
                "markdown": "The WebSocket endpoint `/ws/executions` accepts unauthenticated connections and allows any client to subscribe to arbitrary workflow `executionId` values with no ownership or tenant check. Workflow node events — including full node output (`result.output`, e.g. LLM responses and tool results) — are then broadcast to that client, leaking any tenant's execution data to an unauthenticated network peer.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who knows (or brute-forces/obtains) any execution UUID can stream that execution's live workflow events, including node results produced by LLM and tool nodes, across all organizations/tenants. This exposes other tenants' business data (workflow outputs, error messages, prompts echoed in results) in real time. It also lets an unauthenticated client hold arbitrary subscriptions, enabling resource consumption.\n\nAuthenticate the WebSocket upgrade: validate the JWT (Authorization header or a short-lived one-time ticket issued by an authenticated REST endpoint) inside the `/ws/executions` handler before accepting the connection, and reject unauthenticated upgrades. On `subscribe`, look up the execution by ID scoped to the authenticated user's organization (e.g. `workflowExecutionService.getExecution(executionId, request.user.orgId)`) and reject subscriptions to executions the org does not own. Consider also validating origin headers for browser clients."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-20",
              "name": "Fastify 5.6.2 framework-level validation/auth bypass CVEs (CVE-2026-84469 and related 2026 Fastify advisories)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Fastify 5.6.2 framework-level validation/auth bypass CVEs (CVE-2026-84469 and related 2026 Fastify advisories)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Fastify 5.6.2 (the application's own HTTP framework, a direct production dependency) is affected by multiple published 2026 advisories: request validation bypass (CVE-2026-84469, advisory high), validation bypass via malformed Content-Type leading to integrity impact (CVE-2026-25223), schema validation bypass (CVE-2026-33806), authentication bypass via malformed URLs (CVE-2026-76169), header validation bypass (CVE-2026-84428), and unauthorized state changes/data disclosure via request body replacement (CVE-2026-84504). Fixed in Fastify 5.12.2."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fastify 5.6.2 (the application's own HTTP framework, a direct production dependency) is affected by multiple published 2026 advisories: request validation bypass (CVE-2026-84469, advisory high), validation bypass via malformed Content-Type leading to integrity impact (CVE-2026-25223), schema validation bypass (CVE-2026-33806), authentication bypass via malformed URLs (CVE-2026-76169), header validation bypass (CVE-2026-84428), and unauthorized state changes/data disclosure via request body replacement (CVE-2026-84504). Fixed in Fastify 5.12.2.\n\nRequest-validation and authentication-layer bypasses in the framework that fronts every API route, enabling unauthorized operations and potential data disclosure; also DoS vectors in the router (find-my-way 9.4.0 CVE-2026-47219) and multipart parser (@fastify/busboy 3.2.0, CVE-2026-19481/19484) reachable from unauthenticated request surfaces.\n\nUpgrade fastify to >= 5.12.2, find-my-way (transitive, via fastify) to >= 9.7.0, and @fastify/busboy to >= 3.2.2 (pulled in via @fastify/multipart 9.4.0 used for uploads). Run npm audit / npm update and re-run integration tests.",
                "markdown": "Fastify 5.6.2 (the application's own HTTP framework, a direct production dependency) is affected by multiple published 2026 advisories: request validation bypass (CVE-2026-84469, advisory high), validation bypass via malformed Content-Type leading to integrity impact (CVE-2026-25223), schema validation bypass (CVE-2026-33806), authentication bypass via malformed URLs (CVE-2026-76169), header validation bypass (CVE-2026-84428), and unauthorized state changes/data disclosure via request body replacement (CVE-2026-84504). Fixed in Fastify 5.12.2.\n\nRequest-validation and authentication-layer bypasses in the framework that fronts every API route, enabling unauthorized operations and potential data disclosure; also DoS vectors in the router (find-my-way 9.4.0 CVE-2026-47219) and multipart parser (@fastify/busboy 3.2.0, CVE-2026-19481/19484) reachable from unauthenticated request surfaces.\n\nUpgrade fastify to >= 5.12.2, find-my-way (transitive, via fastify) to >= 9.7.0, and @fastify/busboy to >= 3.2.2 (pulled in via @fastify/multipart 9.4.0 used for uploads). Run npm audit / npm update and re-run integration tests."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-20",
                  "CVE-2026-84469",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1321",
              "name": "axios 1.13.6 (transitive via IM SDKs) — prototype pollution, proxy-credential disclosure, and redirect-handling CVEs (CVE-2026-42264 et al.)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "axios 1.13.6 (transitive via IM SDKs) — prototype pollution, proxy-credential disclosure, and redirect-handling CVEs (CVE-2026-42264 et al.)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "axios 1.13.6 is pinned in the production dependency tree (transitive via @larksuiteoapi/node-sdk, @wecom/aibot-node-sdk, and dingtalk-stream) and is covered by numerous 2026 advisories, including prototype-pollution-based HTTP transport hijacking and header injection (CVE-2026-42033/42035), information disclosure and request manipulation (CVE-2026-42264, CVE-2026-44495), NO_PROXY bypass (CVE-2026-42043), proxy-credential leaks on redirects (CVE-2026-44486/44487), and DoS (CVE-2026-44488). Fixed in 1.16.0."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "axios 1.13.6 is pinned in the production dependency tree (transitive via @larksuiteoapi/node-sdk, @wecom/aibot-node-sdk, and dingtalk-stream) and is covered by numerous 2026 advisories, including prototype-pollution-based HTTP transport hijacking and header injection (CVE-2026-42033/42035), information disclosure and request manipulation (CVE-2026-42264, CVE-2026-44495), NO_PROXY bypass (CVE-2026-42043), proxy-credential leaks on redirects (CVE-2026-44486/44487), and DoS (CVE-2026-44488). Fixed in 1.16.0.\n\nIf any IM adapter passes attacker-influenced values (e.g. webhook URLs, headers from IM event payloads) into SDK HTTP calls, prototype-pollution and redirect-handling flaws could enable request manipulation or credential leakage from outbound IM API calls.\n\nForce-resolve axios to >= 1.16.0 in package-lock (npm overrides / resolution) and audit the IM SDKs for updated releases; verify with `npm ls axios` after regeneration.",
                "markdown": "axios 1.13.6 is pinned in the production dependency tree (transitive via @larksuiteoapi/node-sdk, @wecom/aibot-node-sdk, and dingtalk-stream) and is covered by numerous 2026 advisories, including prototype-pollution-based HTTP transport hijacking and header injection (CVE-2026-42033/42035), information disclosure and request manipulation (CVE-2026-42264, CVE-2026-44495), NO_PROXY bypass (CVE-2026-42043), proxy-credential leaks on redirects (CVE-2026-44486/44487), and DoS (CVE-2026-44488). Fixed in 1.16.0.\n\nIf any IM adapter passes attacker-influenced values (e.g. webhook URLs, headers from IM event payloads) into SDK HTTP calls, prototype-pollution and redirect-handling flaws could enable request manipulation or credential leakage from outbound IM API calls.\n\nForce-resolve axios to >= 1.16.0 in package-lock (npm overrides / resolution) and audit the IM SDKs for updated releases; verify with `npm ls axios` after regeneration."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1321",
                  "CVE-2026-42264",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1321.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "js-yaml 4.1.1 DoS via crafted YAML in user-uploaded API specs (CVE-2026-59869 et al.)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "js-yaml 4.1.1 DoS via crafted YAML in user-uploaded API specs (CVE-2026-59869 et al.)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "js-yaml 4.1.1, a direct production dependency used to parse user-supplied OpenAPI/Swagger specs, is covered by 2026 advisories for denial of service via crafted YAML documents (CVE-2026-59869, fixed 4.3.0) and YAML-parsing DoS (CVE-2026-84375, fixed 4.3.2), plus quadratic CPU consumption in !!omap resolution (GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "js-yaml 4.1.1, a direct production dependency used to parse user-supplied OpenAPI/Swagger specs, is covered by 2026 advisories for denial of service via crafted YAML documents (CVE-2026-59869, fixed 4.3.0) and YAML-parsing DoS (CVE-2026-84375, fixed 4.3.2), plus quadratic CPU consumption in !!omap resolution (GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj).\n\nAn authenticated user can submit a maliciously crafted YAML API spec that causes excessive CPU/memory consumption in the backend process, degrading or denying service for all tenants sharing the process.\n\nUpgrade js-yaml to >= 4.3.2 and add size/complexity limits on uploaded spec documents before parsing.",
                "markdown": "js-yaml 4.1.1, a direct production dependency used to parse user-supplied OpenAPI/Swagger specs, is covered by 2026 advisories for denial of service via crafted YAML documents (CVE-2026-59869, fixed 4.3.0) and YAML-parsing DoS (CVE-2026-84375, fixed 4.3.2), plus quadratic CPU consumption in !!omap resolution (GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj).\n\nAn authenticated user can submit a maliciously crafted YAML API spec that causes excessive CPU/memory consumption in the backend process, degrading or denying service for all tenants sharing the process.\n\nUpgrade js-yaml to >= 4.3.2 and add size/complexity limits on uploaded spec documents before parsing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "CVE-2026-59869",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-532",
              "name": "Output sanitizer redaction gaps: Bearer tokens, connection strings, generic API keys and Windows paths pass through to clients",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Output sanitizer redaction gaps: Bearer tokens, connection strings, generic API keys and Windows paths pass through to clients"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `output-sanitizer` that scrubs agent responses before they are streamed to clients only redacts a narrow set of patterns: POSIX-style server paths, the workspace base dir, home directory, and `AUTH_TOKEN`/AWS key-value strings. Bearer tokens in `Authorization` headers, database connection strings, generic API keys (e.g. `sk-ant-...`), `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`-style env assignments, and Windows paths pass through unredacted into SSE responses rendered to chat clients."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `output-sanitizer` that scrubs agent responses before they are streamed to clients only redacts a narrow set of patterns: POSIX-style server paths, the workspace base dir, home directory, and `AUTH_TOKEN`/AWS key-value strings. Bearer tokens in `Authorization` headers, database connection strings, generic API keys (e.g. `sk-ant-...`), `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`-style env assignments, and Windows paths pass through unredacted into SSE responses rendered to chat clients.\n\nIf an agent (via tool output, file read, or error message) echoes a credential in one of the uncovered forms — an Authorization header, a connection string, a raw API key, or a non-POSIX path — it is delivered verbatim to the chat client. This widens credential-exposure blast radius when agents read config files or invoke authenticated tools.\n\nExtend the pattern set: redact `Bearer\\s+\\S+`, common API-key prefixes (`sk-ant-`, `sk-`, `AKIA`, `ghp_`, `xoxb-`), connection-string credentials (`\\w+://[^:/\\s]+:[^@\\s]+@`), generic `*_API_KEY=`/`*_TOKEN=` assignments, and Windows drive-letter paths (`[A-Za-z]:\\\\`). Prefer an allowlist-style scrub of anything resembling a secret over the current narrow denylist.",
                "markdown": "The `output-sanitizer` that scrubs agent responses before they are streamed to clients only redacts a narrow set of patterns: POSIX-style server paths, the workspace base dir, home directory, and `AUTH_TOKEN`/AWS key-value strings. Bearer tokens in `Authorization` headers, database connection strings, generic API keys (e.g. `sk-ant-...`), `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`-style env assignments, and Windows paths pass through unredacted into SSE responses rendered to chat clients.\n\nIf an agent (via tool output, file read, or error message) echoes a credential in one of the uncovered forms — an Authorization header, a connection string, a raw API key, or a non-POSIX path — it is delivered verbatim to the chat client. This widens credential-exposure blast radius when agents read config files or invoke authenticated tools.\n\nExtend the pattern set: redact `Bearer\\s+\\S+`, common API-key prefixes (`sk-ant-`, `sk-`, `AKIA`, `ghp_`, `xoxb-`), connection-string credentials (`\\w+://[^:/\\s]+:[^@\\s]+@`), generic `*_API_KEY=`/`*_TOKEN=` assignments, and Windows drive-letter paths (`[A-Za-z]:\\\\`). Prefer an allowlist-style scrub of anything resembling a secret over the current narrow denylist."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-532",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "IM webhook signature verification fails open — unauthenticated message injection into agent sessions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IM webhook signature verification fails open — unauthenticated message injection into agent sessions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "IM inbound webhook signature verification fails open: Slack, Telegram, DingTalk, Feishu and WhatsApp adapters return true when no secret is configured, and Discord/WeCom adapters always return true, so unauthenticated POSTs to the public /api/im/* webhook routes are accepted and injected into the org's agent chat pipeline whenever a binding exists without a stored secret."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "IM inbound webhook signature verification fails open: Slack, Telegram, DingTalk, Feishu and WhatsApp adapters return true when no secret is configured, and Discord/WeCom adapters always return true, so unauthenticated POSTs to the public /api/im/* webhook routes are accepted and injected into the org's agent chat pipeline whenever a binding exists without a stored secret.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker who can reach the webhook URLs can inject arbitrary text into the target organization's agent chat sessions. The message is processed with the binding creator's identity and the org's full agent configuration — credentialed agents, tools, MCP servers and workflows act on attacker-chosen instructions. This is a trust-boundary break: the design intends only the verified IM platform to reach this path. It is not a new server-side code-execution primitive by itself.\n\nMake verification fail closed: if no secret is configured for a binding that supports signature verification, reject the inbound message (or refuse to enable the binding until a secret is stored). For adapters that genuinely cannot verify HTTP callbacks (Discord/WeCom gateway modes), disable their dedicated HTTP callback routes or require a per-binding shared secret on the generic /api/im/webhook/:bindingId path instead of returning true. Additionally, scope binding lookup by organization where the route carries org context.",
                "markdown": "IM inbound webhook signature verification fails open: Slack, Telegram, DingTalk, Feishu and WhatsApp adapters return true when no secret is configured, and Discord/WeCom adapters always return true, so unauthenticated POSTs to the public /api/im/* webhook routes are accepted and injected into the org's agent chat pipeline whenever a binding exists without a stored secret.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker who can reach the webhook URLs can inject arbitrary text into the target organization's agent chat sessions. The message is processed with the binding creator's identity and the org's full agent configuration — credentialed agents, tools, MCP servers and workflows act on attacker-chosen instructions. This is a trust-boundary break: the design intends only the verified IM platform to reach this path. It is not a new server-side code-execution primitive by itself.\n\nMake verification fail closed: if no secret is configured for a binding that supports signature verification, reject the inbound message (or refuse to enable the binding until a secret is stored). For adapters that genuinely cannot verify HTTP callbacks (Discord/WeCom gateway modes), disable their dedicated HTTP callback routes or require a per-binding shared secret on the generic /api/im/webhook/:bindingId path instead of returning true. Additionally, scope binding lookup by organization where the route carries org context."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-200",
              "name": "Plaintext IM signing secrets exposed to any org member via GET /api/business-scopes/:scopeId/im-channels (config not redacted)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext IM signing secrets exposed to any org member via GET /api/business-scopes/:scopeId/im-channels (config not redacted)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GET /api/business-scopes/:scopeId/im-channels returns each IM channel binding with its full `config` object included, exposing plaintext platform secrets — Slack `signing_secret`, DingTalk `signing_secret`, Feishu `verification_token`/`encrypt_key`, Telegram `secret_token`, WhatsApp `app_secret`, Discord `public_key`, WeCom `token`/`encoding_aes_key` — to any authenticated member of the organization, while only `bot_token_enc` is masked."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "GET /api/business-scopes/:scopeId/im-channels returns each IM channel binding with its full `config` object included, exposing plaintext platform secrets — Slack `signing_secret`, DingTalk `signing_secret`, Feishu `verification_token`/`encrypt_key`, Telegram `secret_token`, WhatsApp `app_secret`, Discord `public_key`, WeCom `token`/`encoding_aes_key` — to any authenticated member of the organization, while only `bot_token_enc` is masked.\n\nAny member-role user (not just admins) of the organization can read the raw platform verification secrets for every IM binding in the scope. These secrets are exactly the values the inbound webhook signature checks rely on (CWE-312 exposure of the verification credential); possession lets a member forge or validate webhook traffic, and for platforms where the secret is also an outbound credential (e.g. WeCom `encoding_aes_key`, DingTalk app secret) enables impersonation of the bot towards the platform. Confidentiality impact is limited to the organization's own secrets — no cross-tenant access, no server-side state change.\n\nRedact secret-bearing keys from `config` before returning bindings in the list endpoint (and in the create/update responses), the same way `bot_token_enc` is masked: strip or mask keys such as `signing_secret`, `app_secret`, `secret_token`, `verification_token`, `encrypt_key`, `encoding_aes_key`, `token`, `client_secret` unless the caller has an admin role with a documented need. Server-side consumers (verifyAndLookupBinding, adapters) should read the secrets from the database rather than from API responses.",
                "markdown": "GET /api/business-scopes/:scopeId/im-channels returns each IM channel binding with its full `config` object included, exposing plaintext platform secrets — Slack `signing_secret`, DingTalk `signing_secret`, Feishu `verification_token`/`encrypt_key`, Telegram `secret_token`, WhatsApp `app_secret`, Discord `public_key`, WeCom `token`/`encoding_aes_key` — to any authenticated member of the organization, while only `bot_token_enc` is masked.\n\nAny member-role user (not just admins) of the organization can read the raw platform verification secrets for every IM binding in the scope. These secrets are exactly the values the inbound webhook signature checks rely on (CWE-312 exposure of the verification credential); possession lets a member forge or validate webhook traffic, and for platforms where the secret is also an outbound credential (e.g. WeCom `encoding_aes_key`, DingTalk app secret) enables impersonation of the bot towards the platform. Confidentiality impact is limited to the organization's own secrets — no cross-tenant access, no server-side state change.\n\nRedact secret-bearing keys from `config` before returning bindings in the list endpoint (and in the create/update responses), the same way `bot_token_enc` is masked: strip or mask keys such as `signing_secret`, `app_secret`, `secret_token`, `verification_token`, `encrypt_key`, `encoding_aes_key`, `token`, `client_secret` unless the caller has an admin role with a documented need. Server-side consumers (verifyAndLookupBinding, adapters) should read the secrets from the database rather than from API responses."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-367",
              "name": "Non-atomic duplicate-trigger guard in workflow scheduler causes double execution of scheduled workflows",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Non-atomic duplicate-trigger guard in workflow scheduler causes double execution of scheduled workflows"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scheduled-workflow trigger guard in the scheduler tick is a non-atomic check-then-act sequence: concurrent or overlapping ticks can both pass it and execute the same schedule twice, causing duplicate workflow executions and duplicate outbound actions (API calls, notifications)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scheduled-workflow trigger guard in the scheduler tick is a non-atomic check-then-act sequence: concurrent or overlapping ticks can both pass it and execute the same schedule twice, causing duplicate workflow executions and duplicate outbound actions (API calls, notifications).\n\nDuplicate workflow executions: each duplicate run repeats the workflow's action nodes, so outbound api_call requests, messages, or state changes fire twice. For schedules performing payments, notifications, or external mutations this means duplicated real-world side effects. Availability/confidentiality are not affected; integrity impact is limited to duplicated (not arbitrary) actions.\n\nMake the claim of a schedule atomic at the database level: update next_run_at with a conditional update (`WHERE id = ? AND next_run_at = :expectedNow`) and treat zero affected rows as \"another worker claimed it\", or wrap fetch-and-claim in a transaction with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED (PostgreSQL), or use a unique partial index on (schedule_id) WHERE status='running' to reject the second execution. Also serialize ticks within a process by guarding processSchedules with an in-flight flag so a slow tick is not re-entered by the 15-second interval.",
                "markdown": "The scheduled-workflow trigger guard in the scheduler tick is a non-atomic check-then-act sequence: concurrent or overlapping ticks can both pass it and execute the same schedule twice, causing duplicate workflow executions and duplicate outbound actions (API calls, notifications).\n\nDuplicate workflow executions: each duplicate run repeats the workflow's action nodes, so outbound api_call requests, messages, or state changes fire twice. For schedules performing payments, notifications, or external mutations this means duplicated real-world side effects. Availability/confidentiality are not affected; integrity impact is limited to duplicated (not arbitrary) actions.\n\nMake the claim of a schedule atomic at the database level: update next_run_at with a conditional update (`WHERE id = ? AND next_run_at = :expectedNow`) and treat zero affected rows as \"another worker claimed it\", or wrap fetch-and-claim in a transaction with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED (PostgreSQL), or use a unique partial index on (schedule_id) WHERE status='running' to reject the second execution. Also serialize ticks within a process by guarding processSchedules with an in-flight flag so a slow tick is not re-entered by the 15-second interval."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-367",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/367.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-59",
              "name": "Archive symlink members dereferenced by fs.cp enable out-of-bounds file read into skill storage (zip/tar upload)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Archive symlink members dereferenced by fs.cp enable out-of-bounds file read into skill storage (zip/tar upload)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Symlink members inside an uploaded skill archive survive extraction (system `unzip -o` preserves symlinks) and are then dereferenced by `fs.cp` without `verbatimSymlinks` when the skill is copied to its permanent store and later into session workspaces, allowing an attacker to copy arbitrary out-of-archive files and directories into attacker-readable skill storage."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Symlink members inside an uploaded skill archive survive extraction (system `unzip -o` preserves symlinks) and are then dereferenced by `fs.cp` without `verbatimSymlinks` when the skill is copied to its permanent store and later into session workspaces, allowing an attacker to copy arbitrary out-of-archive files and directories into attacker-readable skill storage.\n\nOut-of-bounds file disclosure: any file or directory readable by the backend process user (e.g. `.env`, config files, `data/` contents including other tenants' skill stores, `~/.aws/credentials` if readable) is copied into the attacker's skill directory, where it is retrievable via skill download, workspace provisioning, or the skill listing. A directory symlink whose target is writable also creates a write pivot: extraction of a later archive (or the copy itself) can write through the symlink into the targeted directory. Integrity impact is limited (writes through a pre-planted writable directory symlink); no availability impact.\n\nPass `verbatimSymlinks: true` to every `fs.cp` that copies extracted archive content (the permanent-store copy in `installFromZip`, and the workspace provisioning copies in `installSkillToWorkspace` and `downloadSkill`), then reject or strip any remaining symlinks in extracted skill directories. Preferably, reject any archive containing symlink or hardlink members at ingest time, mirroring the existing `..` entry protections, and validate post-extraction that no entry is a link before copying.",
                "markdown": "Symlink members inside an uploaded skill archive survive extraction (system `unzip -o` preserves symlinks) and are then dereferenced by `fs.cp` without `verbatimSymlinks` when the skill is copied to its permanent store and later into session workspaces, allowing an attacker to copy arbitrary out-of-archive files and directories into attacker-readable skill storage.\n\nOut-of-bounds file disclosure: any file or directory readable by the backend process user (e.g. `.env`, config files, `data/` contents including other tenants' skill stores, `~/.aws/credentials` if readable) is copied into the attacker's skill directory, where it is retrievable via skill download, workspace provisioning, or the skill listing. A directory symlink whose target is writable also creates a write pivot: extraction of a later archive (or the copy itself) can write through the symlink into the targeted directory. Integrity impact is limited (writes through a pre-planted writable directory symlink); no availability impact.\n\nPass `verbatimSymlinks: true` to every `fs.cp` that copies extracted archive content (the permanent-store copy in `installFromZip`, and the workspace provisioning copies in `installSkillToWorkspace` and `downloadSkill`), then reject or strip any remaining symlinks in extracted skill directories. Preferably, reject any archive containing symlink or hardlink members at ingest time, mirroring the existing `..` entry protections, and validate post-extraction that no entry is a link before copying."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-59",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/59.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "strix-coverage/infrastructure-security-sg-exposure-iam-wildcards-s3-public-access-tls-vpc-placement",
              "name": "Infrastructure security: SG exposure, IAM wildcards, S3 public access, TLS, VPC placement",
