          "ruleId": "vuln-0017",
          "ruleIndex": 14,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Agent runtime shell guard is a bypassable denylist under permissionMode bypassPermissions (prompt-injection path to command execution)\n\nThe Claude agent runtime runs with `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`, granting the model Bash, Write, Edit, and WebFetch by default. The only compensating control on shell commands is a small regex denylist (`dangerousCommandBlocker`) that is trivially bypassed via variable indirection, base64 wrapping, interpreters, or relative paths. Untrusted content that reaches an agent conversation (IM messages, attached files, retrieved documents) can therefore drive arbitrary command execution, file writes outside intended flows, and outbound exfiltration under the agent's credentials — with no human-confirmation gate."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "super-agent/backend",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6eb0108c05627f4b52322e2e010a7f438721551f1d537e4d815d63458f2cdf8d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "10.0",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "e51ac05eb841faaa16bf654781884eebd67ac7013eae20cc2a856c761d0d379f",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 10.0,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-21 04:10:51 UTC",
              "target": "super-agent/backend",
              "impact": "An attacker who injects instructions into agent context (IM file content, chat, fetched web pages, skill files) gets arbitrary command execution in the agent's execution environment and exfiltration of any file the process can read (workspace files, and in local mode the backend process's environment). The agent holds a member-role platform JWT (AUTH_TOKEN, claude-agent.service.ts:479-485) and, in Bedrock mode, AWS credentials, so successful injection escalates to those credentials. There is no approval gate for destructive actions; the workflow humanApproval node exists only inside workflow executions, not chat/IM sessions.",
              "technical_analysis": "Root cause chain:\n\n1. `src/services/claude-agent.service.ts:419-437` — `buildOptions` sets `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'`, `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions: true`, and `allowedTools: ['Bash','Read','Write','Edit','Glob','Grep','WebFetch','Skill']` (line 219). The SDK therefore executes every tool call without user confirmation.\n2. `src/services/claude-hooks.ts:53-70` — the only shell control, `DANGEROUS_PATTERNS` (5 regexes) and `PATH_ESCAPE_PATTERNS`, matches literal substrings. Bypasses demonstrated in a local unit run: variable indirection (`X=/; rm -rf $X`), base64-wrapped payloads (`echo ... | base64 -d | sh`), command substitution (`sh -c \"$(curl ...)\"`), interpreter execution (`node -e`), exfiltration without a pipe (`curl -d @/etc/passwd`), and workspace destruction via relative path (`rm -rf ./`). The path check also only matches a fixed list of directory prefixes and `..` sequences, and only inspects `file_path`/`path` tool inputs (the WebFetch `url` field is entirely unchecked).\n3. The runtime injects a member-role platform JWT as `AUTH_TOKEN` and the full `process.env` (plus Bedrock keys) into the subprocess environment (`claude-agent.service.ts:462-490`), so any command the model runs inherits these credentials.\n4. Untrusted input reaches these sessions with no content-isolation: IM file attachments are written into the agent workspace and referenced in the prompt (`im.service.ts:222-244`), chat and room messages feed the same runtime, and WebFetch results enter the conversation.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the denylist with an allowlist-based execution policy: run agent sessions in a hardened sandbox (container/namespace with no ambient credentials, restricted egress), drop `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` and `allowDangerouslySkipPermissions` in favor of explicit per-tool approval or a strict allowlist of permitted command prefixes and workspace-relative paths resolved after shell-expansion; block network egress from Bash/WebFetch except to operator-configured destinations; and add a human-confirmation gate for destructive tool calls triggered from IM-sourced conversations. Do not pass the full `process.env` or a member-role platform JWT to the agent subprocess.",
              "counterevidence": "Controls that exist: the skill allowlist hook (createSkillAccessChecker) is a real allowlist and works; workspace per-session isolation limits cross-session file access; binary read blocker prevents binary corruption. However, the Bash guard is the only shell control and it is denylist-based; I verified six distinct bypasses against the actual hook implementation in a local unit run, including variable indirection, base64 wrapping, and interpreter-based execution. The `<security>` prompt directive is advisory text with no enforcement. No OS-level sandboxing, seccomp, container isolation, or egress restriction was found for the local claude runtime path.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that production deployments run the Claude runtime inside a network-isolated, credential-free sandbox would lower the impact to workspace-local integrity and drop severity substantially; conversely a demonstrated prompt-injection payload reaching a bound IM channel would make this fully end-to-end exploitable as rated.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Import the repo's own hook function and feed it Bash tool_input strings an injected prompt would cause the model to run.\n2. Observe that only literal patterns like `rm -rf /`, `dd if=/dev/zero`, `curl ... | bash` and absolute paths into a fixed directory list are blocked; every obfuscated variant returns no block decision.\n3. Since buildOptions sets permissionMode 'bypassPermissions' and allowDangerouslySkipPermissions, the SDK does not prompt the user for these tool calls, so an ALLOWED decision executes unconditionally.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
