          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 13,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Cross-tenant access-control gaps in scope memberships, scope plugin/MCP listings, and scope connector bindings\n\nThree authorization gaps around business scopes: (1) PATCH/DELETE /api/business-scopes/:id/members/:membershipId update or delete a scope membership by primary key only, letting a scope-admin in Org A grant themselves any role in (or strip members from) any scope in Org B; (2) GET /api/business-scopes/:id/plugins and /:id/mcp-servers return cross-tenant data via raw SQL with no organization predicate; (3) GET /api/data-connectors/scopes/:scopeId/connectors returns another org's connectors and their configurations."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "super-agent/backend (local repository /workspace/agentcore-superagent-update)",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "bc7bf994fb6dde7a550a499998ef24b312dd47264e4d0500a57613fd83ca93ca"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "99bdccedad1f0a3bbeefddd4c11606cae91e20f8cd5d178e435661b42eeea53c",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0018",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-21 04:10:58 UTC",
              "target": "super-agent/backend (local repository /workspace/agentcore-superagent-update)",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "Cross-tenant privilege escalation (attacker scope-admin elevates their own or any account's role in a victim org's restricted scope, gaining access to that scope's agents, data connectors, document groups and chat workflows), cross-tenant data disclosure (MCP server host addresses and scope configs, plugin git URLs, connector configurations), and cross-tenant state tampering (visibility changes and membership removal).",
              "technical_analysis": "Root cause 1 — scopeMemberships.routes.ts:122-127 and 137-142 call `scopeAccessService.updateMemberRole(membershipId, role)` / `removeMember(membershipId)`, which delegate to `scopeMembershipRepository.updateRole` (scopeMembership.repository.ts:86-91) and `.delete` (:96-98). Both issue `prisma.scope_memberships.update/delete({ where: { id } })` with no organization predicate. The requireScopeAccess('admin') guard only validates the `:id` scope param against the attacker's org — the `:membershipId` row is never tied back to that scope or org. Root cause 2 — businessScopes.routes.ts GET '/:id/plugins' (:815-833) and GET '/:id/mcp-servers' (:935-953) execute raw SQL filtering only on `business_scope_id`; the sibling endpoints '/:id/agents' and '/:id/skills' do call scopeAccessService.requireAccess, these two do not. Root cause 3 — scopeMemberships.routes.ts:150-171 `business_scopes.update({ where: { id } })` for visibility has no org predicate (unlike the adjacent raw-SQL config_version bump which includes organization_id). Root cause 4 — connectors.routes.ts:126-129 passes scopeId to `getScopeConnectors` which queries `scope_data_connectors` by business_scope_id only (data-connector.repository.ts:106-113), including the full connector record.",
              "remediation_steps": "Resolve the membership row (or scope) first and verify its organization_id equals request.user.orgId inside updateMemberRole/removeMember/visibility handlers; add organization_id join predicates to the raw SQL plugin/MCP-server listings and to findScopeConnectors; scope the scope_mcp_servers delete/config updates by joining business_scopes.organization_id rather than only checking scope ownership on a separate statement.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether the requireScopeAccess('admin') preHandler on PATCH/DELETE :id/members/:membershipId verifies the target membership's org: the middleware's defaultExtractor reads only params.id/params.scopeId, and scopeAccessService.getAccessLevel scopes the check to the user's own org — so passing a victim-org scopeId in :id correctly fails, which is why the attack targets :membershipId instead. The raw-SQL GET endpoints have no scopeAccessService call at all (contrast with /:id/agents and /:id/skills which call it), confirming these were simply missed.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that restricted scopes hold regulated data or that scope_config embeds credentials for MCP servers would raise impact; a database-level tenant RLS policy would lower it.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as a scope-admin in Org A (admin of any scope in your org to pass requireScopeAccess('admin')). 2. PATCH /api/business-scopes/:myScopeId/members/:victimMembershipId with {role:'admin'} — updateMemberRole updates scope_memberships by {id} with no org predicate, elevating the attacker's account to admin of the victim org's restricted scope. 3. DELETE the same endpoint with a victim membership id to evict members from another org's scope. 4. GET /api/business-scopes/:victimScopeId/plugins and /:victimScopeId/mcp-servers — raw SQL selects by business_scope_id only, returning the victim org's MCP server host addresses, configs and plugin git URLs. 5. GET /api/data-connectors/scopes/:victimScopeId/connectors returns the victim org's connectors including scope_config. 6. PATCH /api/business-scopes/:victimScopeId/visibility flips another org's scope between open/restricted.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
