          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "73f4acd73d3264f42e2c2b689a5cf7f2a3c67217168fb48cbe4a4ff3b8126cad"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "08c50067f94a373d3e2f615f7c5d4aebffe2a8a01eed30b9e8600c1d096ee562",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0020",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-21 04:11:20 UTC",
              "target": "super-agent/backend",
              "cwe": "CWE-306",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker who knows (or brute-forces/obtains) any execution UUID can stream that execution's live workflow events, including node results produced by LLM and tool nodes, across all organizations/tenants. This exposes other tenants' business data (workflow outputs, error messages, prompts echoed in results) in real time. It also lets an unauthenticated client hold arbitrary subscriptions, enabling resource consumption.",
              "technical_analysis": "`ExecutionWebSocketGateway.register()` (src/websocket/execution.gateway.ts:126-141) registers the route with only `{ websocket: true }` and no `preHandler`/auth. The backend applies authentication exclusively as a per-route `preHandler: [authenticate]` on REST routes; there is no global auth hook (verified in src/app.ts — only request-logging and audit onResponse hooks are global), so the WS route is unauthenticated. `handleConnection()` (line 155) never inspects the request. `handleSubscribe()` (lines 190-236) adds the client to `this.subscriptions` for any non-empty string `executionId` with no check that the execution belongs to the connecting user's organization. The bridge in src/setup/event-websocket-bridge.ts:46 forwards every `workflow:event` from `workflowExecutionService` — including `node:completed`/`node:paused` events whose `data.result` carries the full node output (`result.output`, src/services/workflow-execution.service.ts:459-483) — to all subscribers of that executionId.",
              "remediation_steps": "Authenticate the WebSocket upgrade: validate the JWT (Authorization header or a short-lived one-time ticket issued by an authenticated REST endpoint) inside the `/ws/executions` handler before accepting the connection, and reject unauthenticated upgrades. On `subscribe`, look up the execution by ID scoped to the authenticated user's organization (e.g. `workflowExecutionService.getExecution(executionId, request.user.orgId)`) and reject subscriptions to executions the org does not own. Consider also validating origin headers for browser clients.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked for controls that could mitigate: (1) no global onRequest auth hook exists in app.ts — authentication is exclusively per-route preHandler, and the WS route registers none; (2) execution IDs are UUIDs, so an attacker must know/guess one — this raises difficulty but execution IDs appear in API responses to any org member and can be enumerated from prior executions; (3) no origin/Referer or token validation occurs anywhere in the gateway (searched whole file). Mitigation via obscurity of UUIDs is the only friction found; it does not prevent cross-tenant subscription when an ID is known.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Working local PoC against the actual gateway module plus a complete code trace showing no auth hook on the route and no ownership checks in handleSubscribe. Runtime validation used an isolated localhost instance (per assessment constraints), not the deployed service.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that execution IDs are high-entropy UUIDs never exposed cross-tenant and that the backend port is not network-reachable would lower severity; conversely, evidence that node results routinely contain credentials/PII would keep it high.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Start the backend (or a Fastify instance with the gateway registered) on a reachable host.\n2. Open a WebSocket to `ws://<host>:<port>/ws/executions` with no Authorization header or token.\n3. Send `{\"type\":\"subscribe\",\"executionId\":\"<any execution UUID>\"}`.\n4. Observe the server replies `{\"type\":\"subscribed\",...}` and subsequently delivers `workflow_event` messages containing that execution's node status and result data.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
